{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Alignment Grid Spacing}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Alignment_Grid_Spacing}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Alignment Grid Spacing
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par An Alignment Grid pattern can be defined. Objects on the Overlay Plane can be aligned to the grid raster. This helps to make a picture look well structured especially if may objects are shown.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm7.bmp\} You must be in  \b Annotation Mode.\plain\fs24 
\par 
\par \{bmc bm8.bmp\}\tab \b Open the Properties Dialog\plain\fs24  by \ul clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} with the right mouse button.
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx7935\cellx12115\pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select \b Grid Settings\plain\fs24 . The Panel opens.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Type in the \b Spacings \plain\fs24 in \b Width\plain\fs24  and \b Height\plain\fs24  in number of pixels.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select \b Snap to Grid\plain\fs24  in Options if you want all objects to snap to the grid automatically.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab With \b <OK>\plain\fs24  the new grid setting is accepted.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab \b <Cancel>\plain\fs24  terminates the panel without change. 
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl\tx355 \par\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm9.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Alignment of Annotation Objects}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Alignment_of_Annotation_Objects}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Adjustable Width Measurement}
\b\fs36\cf1 Alignment of Annotation Objects
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par Annotation objects can be aligned laterally by snapping them to an Alignment Grid.
\par 
\par They can also be aligned in layers where partial overlap may be present. They can be brought forward or set back. 
\par 
\par \{bmc bm10.bmp\}\tab \b Select the object\plain\fs24  by \ul clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} with the left mouse button on it. A selected object displays \ul handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles@Leo_1500.HLP}.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm8.bmp\}\tab \b Open the Properties Dialog\plain\fs24  by clicking with the right mouse button.
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab To align the marked object(s) laterally click on \b Snap to Grid\plain\fs24 . \uldb \b Alignment Grid Spacing\plain\b\fs24 {\v Alignment_Grid_Spacing}\plain\fs24  can be adjusted.
\par 
\par \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select \b Bring to Front\plain\fs24  to bring the marked object(s) on top of all others.
\par 
\par \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select \b Bring Forward\plain\fs24  to move the marked object(s) on level to the front.
\par 
\par \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select  \b Send to Back\plain\fs24  to take the marked object(s) the furthest behind.
\par 
\par \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select \b Send Back\plain\fs24  to move the marked object(s) one level back.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par \{bmc bm4.bmp\}\tab  Depending on the relative level with respect to other layers \b Bring to Front\plain\fs24  and \b Bring Forward\plain\fs24  or \b Send to Back\plain\fs24  and \b Send Back\plain\fs24  may not be available.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Angular Measurement}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 4QHH_A0}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 \{bmc bm11.bmp\}  Angular Measurement
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 The angular measurement facility comprises a related pair of measurement lines, a \b reference line\plain\fs24  (identified by the symbol \b AaRn\plain\fs24 ) and a \b measurement line\plain\fs24  (identified by \b Aan\plain\fs24 )\b .\plain\fs24  Each line has a 'blob' at the end which identities it's centre of rotation. Each line may be adjusted in length, angle and position. \uldb \b Precision Adjustment\plain\b\fs24 {\v Precision_Adjustment}\plain\fs24  is available
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx9065\cellx13255\pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\tab \plain\ul\fs24 Click\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} on the \b Angular Measurement Icon \plain\fs24 to switch the function on.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Point to the \b Zone\plain\fs24  for which you want to place the Angular Measurement Markers and click.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Pick the \ul handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles@Sem.HLP} of the \b Reference Line AaRn\plain\fs24  and place them.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Pick the handles of the \b Measurement Line Aan\plain\fs24  and place them. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Double clicking on one of the lines calls the \uldb \b Results Panel\plain\b\fs24 {\v Results_Panel_Parameters}\plain\fs24  loaded from file \b An.ANN\plain\fs24  containing the measurement parameter \b Aa n \plain\fs24 the angle between the lines where \b n\plain\fs24  is the instance identifier (i.e. 1 for first object created, 2 for second object)
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab The data from this objects \uldb results panel\plain\fs24 {\v Results_Panel_Parameters} can be copied to the clipboard for insertion into a speadsheet. This is achieved using the \uldb Annotation Copy\plain\fs24 {\v Anntotation_to_Clipboard} Popup Menu item.
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl\tx355 \par\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \{bmc bm12.bmp\}\par\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \par\pard \pard\intbl Note: If in \ul reduced format\plain\fs24 {\v Reduced_Format_display_of_results} the acute angle between the lines is displayed instead of the angle relative to the reference line\cell\intbl\row
\pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 See also\tab \uldb \b Properties of Angular Measurement\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Angular_Measurement}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Annotating the Image}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Annotating_the_Image}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Alignment Grid}
\b\fs36\cf1 Annotating the Image
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par Annotation can be performed in several ways:
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx845\cellx13535\pard\intbl \{bmc bm13.bmp\} \cell\pard \pard\intbl \uldb \b Text Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Text_Annotation} \plain\fs24  to add text, current date, current time, and user name to the Image Overlay plane. Font, styles and colours may be defined for each individual text string. The function can be directly called with \b <CTRL + T>.\plain\fs24 
\par\intbl For properties like font, background, alignment of text see \uldb \b Properties of Text Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Text_Annotation}\plain\fs24 \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx845\cellx13535\pard\intbl \b \{bmc bm14.bmp\} \plain\fs24 \cell\pard \pard\intbl \uldb \b Parameter Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Parameter_Annotation} \plain\fs24 allows any parameter of the SEM to be included in the annotation overlay.
\par\intbl For properties like font, background, alignment of parameter text see\b  \uldb Properties of Parameter Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Parameter_Annotation}\plain\fs24 \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx845\cellx13535\pard\intbl \{bmc bm15.bmp\}\cell\pard \pard\intbl \uldb \b Insert User Bitmap or Metafile\plain\b\fs24 {\v Insert_User_Bitmap_or_Metafile} \plain\fs24 allows to insert bitmaps or metafiles which the user generated like logos or other special signs.
\par\intbl For properties like background or alignment of the picture see\b  \uldb Properties of Bitmap Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Bitmap_Annotation} \plain\fs24 \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx845\cellx13535\pard\intbl \b \{bmc bm16.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \cell\pard \pard\intbl \uldb \b Line Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Line_Annotation} \plain\fs24 adds a line to the overlay plane. The line may be configured as an arrow.
\par\intbl For properties like pen setting, arrow head or alignment of the line see\b  \uldb Properties of Line Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Line_Annotation}\plain\fs24 \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx845\cellx13535\pard\intbl \b \{bmc bm17.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \cell\pard \pard\intbl \uldb \b Rectangle Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Rectangle_Annotation} \plain\fs24 adds a rectangle to the overlay plane. The rectangle may be filled or transparent.
\par\intbl For properties like pen setting, brush setting or alignment of the rectangle see\b  \uldb Properties of Rectangle Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Rectangle_Annotation}\plain\fs24 \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx845\cellx13535\pard\intbl \b \{bmc bm18.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \cell\pard \pard\intbl \uldb \b Ellipse Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Ellipse_Annotation} \plain\fs24 adds an ellipse to the overlay plane. The ellipse may be filled or transparent.
\par\intbl For properties like pen setting, brush setting or alignment of the ellipse see \uldb \b Properties of Ellipse Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Ellipse_Annotation}\plain\fs24 \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx845\cellx13535\pard\intbl \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm19.bmp\}\cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl\tx355 \uldb \b Sticky Panel Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Sticky_Panel_Annotation} \plain\fs24 adds a rectangle to the overlay plane. The rectangle may be filled or transparent.\par\pard \pard\intbl For properties like pen setting, brush setting or alignment of the rectangle see\b  \uldb Properties of Sticky Panel Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Sticky_Panel_Annotation}\plain\fs24 \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx845\cellx13535\pard\intbl \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm20.bmp\}\cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl\tx355 \uldb \b TIFF Data Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v TIFF_Data_Annotation}\plain\fs24  displays data which have been saved with a TIFF file and retrieved again.\par\pard \pard\intbl For properties like font, background, alignment see \uldb \b Properties of TIFF Data Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_TIFF_Data_Annotation}\plain\fs24 \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx845\cellx13535\pard\intbl \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl \uldb \b Data Zone\plain\b\fs24 {\v Data_Zone}\plain\fs24  is a panel with annotation placed upon it, and as such it may be edited as any other annotation panel. However Data Zone design can be individually saved by the user and loaded back again for later use.\cell\intbl\row
\pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par \{bmc bm4.bmp\}\tab \b Note : \plain\fs24 All annotation and measurement objects created on screen can be saved or printed with the image by selecting the Overlay plane On from the Export TIFF Setup menu.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Annotation File Types}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Annotation_File_Types}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Annotation File Types \fs28 (.ANN, .ADZ)\fs36 
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par \uldb \b Annotation Objects\plain\b\fs24 {\v Object_Selection}\plain\fs24  that can be saved is stored in \b .ANN\plain\fs24  files along with their respective \uldb \b properties\plain\b\fs24 {\v Object_Properties}\plain\fs24   (some objects such as the \uldb \b reduced raster\plain\b\fs24 {\v Reduced_Raster_Object}\plain\fs24  box cannot be saved as annotation). This may be useful if a specific overlay is required to load on top of an image in the SEM Image Store, or if a frequently used set of measurement objects needs to be created more easily.
\par 
\par The reason for the Annotation having two file extensions is that if the user wishes to load in a \i special\plain\fs24  user defined \uldb \b DataZone\plain\b\fs24 {\v Data_Zone}\plain\fs24 , then the definition for the DataZone is saved as an \b .ADZ\plain\fs24  file. If the file is saved as\b  CURRENT.ADZ\plain\fs24  in the User Directory, then when the DataZone is displayed from the SEM User Interface this annotation file is loaded. The internal formats of the \b .ADZ\plain\fs24  and \b .ANN\plain\fs24  files are otherwise identical.
\par \pard 
\par \ul Note :\plain\fs24  As this file is automatically saved when any changes are made to the current DataZone, \i do not\plain\fs24  copy a user defined DataZone over \b CURRENT.ADZ\plain\fs24  \i while the SEM User Interface is loaded\plain\fs24 . This is because at the next DataZone change, \b CURRENT.ADZ\plain\fs24  will be overwritten with whatever is currently in memory, overwriting any \b CURRENT.ADZ\plain\fs24  file already present on disk.
\par \page
