{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Clipboard Copy}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 2OP8_K6}
\pard\keepn\tx355 {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Anntotation to Clipboard}
\fs36\cf1 Clipboard Copy
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 When the \b Copy\plain\fs24  tab of the \uldb \b Clipboard dialog\plain\b\fs24 {\v Export_to_the_System_Clipboard}\plain\fs24  is displayed the operator is able to define whether to include annotation with the image and what area of the image should be copied.
\par \pard\tx355 \{bmc bm31.bmp\}\tab The Image Window should be frozen before attempting to copy to the clipboard.
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx13815\pard\intbl Select the \ul \b\ul Store Resolution\plain\b\fs24 {\v Store_resolution}\plain\fs24  from the Drop-down List. which selects the pixel density of the copied image.
\par\intbl Annotation, Overlay and Color can be \ul merged\plain\fs24 {\v merge_settings} into the image if selected by \{bmc bm32.bmp\}.
\par\intbl If \b As seen\plain\fs24  is checked \{bmc bm32.bmp\} the image data copied must first be transformed using the RED portion of the display LUT.
\par\intbl A \b Reduction\plain\fs24  between 1 (no reduction) and 8 may be selected from the Drop-down List.
\par\intbl If the check box labeled \b Whole\plain\fs24  has been checked \{bmc bm32.bmp\},
\par\intbl the whole screen area, which will be overlayed blue, will be copied as the image.\cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm33.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx13815\pard\intbl If this check box is not checked, then the operator can define the area to be saved by resizing the area of the blue box. using the \ul handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles}. or setting the dimensions in the \b Dimensions\plain\fs24  box by typing values for X (start x), Y (start y), W (width), and H (height) and click on \b Set\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl Clicking on the \b Centre\plain\fs24  button will position the defined area about the centre of the screen.
\par\intbl To terminate the clipboard without copying click on \{bmc bm24.bmp\}.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl See \ul \b\ul Annotation Warning\plain\b\fs24 {\v Annotation_Warning}\plain\fs24 
\par\intbl See\uldb \b  Paste\plain\b\fs24 {\v Clipboard_Paste}\plain\fs24 \cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm34.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Clipboard Paste}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Clipboard_Paste}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Clipboard Paste
\par \pard \plain\fs24 When the \b Paste\plain\fs24  tab of the \uldb \b Clipboard dialog\plain\b\fs24 {\v Export_to_the_System_Clipboard}\plain\fs24  is displayed the operator is able to select to where the image should be pasted.
\par \{bmc bm31.bmp\}  Before an image may be pasted the image store must be frozen.
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx13815\pard\intbl From the Drop-down List the \ul \b\ul resolution\plain\b\fs24 {\v Store_resolution}\plain\fs24  for pasting the image in the image store can be selected.
\par\intbl Also the \b Image Reduction\plain\fs24  may be selected from another Drop-down List.
\par\intbl The screen area, where the image will be imported to is marked in red.
\par\intbl \b Load at \plain\fs24 allows to select \b Centre \plain\fs24 where the pasted image is centered in the Image Window. 
\par\intbl \b Origin\plain\fs24  places it at the screen origin, and \b X,Y\plain\fs24  allows to set individual coordinates.\cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm35.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx13815\pard\intbl \{bmc bm10.bmp\}  Also the area may be dragged with the mouse to a position.
\par\intbl If \b Step Frame\plain\fs24  is checked \{bmc bm32.bmp\} the pasted image will be offset by a frame width or frame height after pasting.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl When all parameters are set, press \b <Paste>\plain\fs24  to actually paste the image.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl To terminate the clipboard without pasting click on \{bmc bm24.bmp\}.\cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm36.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard See also\uldb \b  Copy\plain\b\fs24 {\v Clipboard_Copy}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Co-ordinate System}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Co_ordinate_System}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Co-ordinate System
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par Although the store resolution of the SEM Image can be greater or less than the viewport area, Image Manager always works over a 1024x768 display area. 
\par 
\par This is an important point, as all cartesian co-ordinates used by Image Manager are also in these display units.
\par 
\par The top left co-ordinate (0,0) represents the origin of the display. 
\par 
\par The bottom right limit of the display area is therefore at co-ordinate (1023, 767).
\par \page
