{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Data Zone}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Data_Zone}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Data Zone
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par The data zone is a special group of annotation objects which are used to display current parameters, including if required a micron marker showing the base magnification. The data zone objects are disconnected from the normal annotation objects (e.g. reset does not remove them) and they are switched on/off using the top menu function from \uldb \b Menu View\plain\b\fs24 {\v 6Y3XP3@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par \{bmc bm42.bmp\}
\par 
\par The user may edit the data zone by using the technique of \uldb \b Sticky Panel Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Sticky_Panel_Annotation} \plain\fs24 .
\par \pard 
\par Modifications to the Data Zone, for example the addition of \uldb \b SEM Parameter Objects\plain\b\fs24 {\v SEM_Parameter_Object}\plain\fs24 , will be saved automatically.
\par 
\par When a Data Zone is subsequently recalled it is loaded from a \uldb \b CURRENT.ADZ\plain\b\fs24 {\v Annotation_File_Types}\plain\fs24  file in the users directory.
\par 
\par \pard\li225 \ul\fs18 Note :\plain\fs18  As this file is automatically saved when any changes are made to the current DataZone, \i do not\plain\fs18  copy a user defined DataZone over \b CURRENT.ADZ\plain\fs18  \i while the SEM User Interface is loaded\plain\fs18 . This is because at the next DataZone change, \b CURRENT.ADZ\plain\fs18  will be overwritten with whatever is currently in memory, overwriting any \b CURRENT.ADZ\plain\fs18  file already present on disk.
\par \fs24 
\par User Datazones \uldb \b saved\plain\b\fs24 {\v Save_As_Datazone}\plain\fs24  in a files may be loaded from the Main Interface Menu \b View - Datazones - Load User Datazone.\plain\fs18 
\par \pard\li225 
\par \pard\tx355 \fs24 \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab \b Note: If the data zone is to be used for annotation of hard copy and the image has been loaded from TIFF file then only those objects which reflect the data in the TIFF file should be used (\uldb TIFF data\plain\b\fs24 {\v TIFF_Data_Annotation} , \uldb Retrospective Measurement\plain\b\fs24 {\v Retrospective_Measurement})
\par 
\par \plain\fs24 See also\b  \uldb How to Create a datazone\plain\b\fs24 {\v O3YXGB@how_to.hlp} \plain\fs24 and\b  \uldb How To Edit a Datazone\plain\b\fs24 {\v 2E164BN@how_to.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Delete All Seen}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Delete_All_Seen}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Calibration Lines}
\b\fs36\cf1 Delete All Seen
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par Deletes all visible \uldb \b Annotation Objects\plain\b\fs24 {\v Delete_all_Visible_Objects}\plain\fs24  from the ovelay plane.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab The \uldb \b Data Zone\plain\b\fs24 {\v Data_Zone}\plain\fs24  is not deleted.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab Deletion can be reversed with \uldb \b Undo Last Edit/Delete\plain\b\fs24 {\v Undo_Last_Edit_Delete}\plain\fs24 . 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Delete all Visible Objects}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Delete_all_Visible_Objects}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 \{bmc bm43.bmp\}  Delete all Visible Objects
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \{bmc bm10.bmp\}\tab \ul Clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} on the \b Delete all Visible Objects\plain\fs24  icon clears the complete overlay plane (annotations, measurements), and then the icon is disabled (greyed).
\par 
\par \{bmc bm4.bmp\}\tab The icon is only active if annotation objects are present in the overlay plane.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm31.bmp\}\tab If you have deleted the overlay plane by accident the Undo Last Edit function \{bmc bm44.bmp\} brings it back.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Deleting of Annotation}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Deleting_of_Annotation}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Calling the Annotation Panel}
\b\fs36\cf1 Deleting of Annotation
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par The annotation object can be deleted:
\par 
\par \{bmc bm10.bmp\}\tab \b Select the object\plain\fs24  by \ul clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} with the left mouse button on it. A selected object displays \ul handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles@Sem.HLP}.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm28.bmp\}\tab The direct command (Short Cut) from the Keyboard is \b <DEL>\plain\fs24  \b  \plain\fs24 to delete the annotation object
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \{bmc bm8.bmp\}\tab \b Open the Properties Dialog\plain\fs24  by clicking with the right mouse button.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm29.bmp\}\tab Click on \b Delete \plain\fs24 to delete the annotation object.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par 
\par \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab \b Warning: \plain\fs24 Make sure that you have selected the object you want to delete. Otherwise another item which was previously selected may be deleted.
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \{bmc bm4.bmp\} Some objects such as the \uldb \b Data Zone\plain\b\fs24 {\v Data_Zone}\plain\fs24 , or the \uldb \b Reduced Raster\plain\b\fs24 {\v BFKLC0@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  objects for example will not be affected by this function. \uldb \b Hidden Objects\plain\b\fs24 {\v Hidden_Objects}\plain\fs24  are also unaffected by this action.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \{bmc bm31.bmp\} Use the \uldb \b Undo\plain\b\fs24 {\v Undo_Last_Edit}\plain\fs24  function to restore any mistakenly deleted annotation.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Direction Arrow Property}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Direction_Arrow_Property}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Direction Arrow Property
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par This property defines whether or not an arrow-head is drawn on a \uldb \b Line Object\plain\b\fs24 {\v Line_Annotation}\plain\fs24 . 
\par 
\par The end of the line at which the arrow head is drawn is determined by the first point clicked when creating the line object.
\par 
\par The arrow head orientation automatically compensates for the angle of the line.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Dual Magnification Object}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Dual_Magnification_Object}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Caption Property}
\b\fs36\cf1 Dual Magnification Object
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par This object is a 'mode graphic' and simply exists to depict boundaries of the SEM \uldb \b Dual Magnification Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v OMU1V2@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 . 
\par 
\par \{bmc bm27.bmp\}  This function can be selected from the SEM \uldb \b Menu Bar\plain\b\fs24 {\v 8ZP0K4@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 . 
\par 
\par The colour of this graphic can be changed from the \uldb \b Annotation Settings Popup Menu\plain\b\fs24 {\v Annotation_Settings}\plain\fs24  under \uldb \b Selection of Pen Settings\plain\b\fs24 {\v Selection_of_Pen_Settings}\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par The size of the adjustment box displayed depicts the zoom factor applied to the right hand side of the \uldb \b Split Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1QEXFRQ@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  image.
\par \pard 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx9635\cellx12735\pard\intbl The \uldb \b selected object\plain\b\fs24 {\v Object_Selection}\plain\fs24  can be reseized by \ul dragging\plain\fs24 {\v DRAG@Sem.HLP} its selection \ul handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles@Sem.HLP} to the desired object size, or moved by \ul clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} on one of the objects lines and dragging the mouse. After the mouse button is released, the SEM Dual Magnification Mode parameters are set to these new values.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl When selected, this object can be \uldb \b deleted\plain\b\fs24 {\v Deleting_of_Annotation}\plain\fs24  by using its \uldb \b Properties\plain\b\fs24 {\v Objects_Properties_Menu}\plain\fs24  popup menu. Deleting this object sets the SEM to \uldb \b Normal Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v 4LTP_XJ@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b Special Features\plain\fs24 
\par\intbl \par\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \fs28 \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\fs24 \tab When adjusting the objects selection handles, the Dual Mag Object scales from its centre point, altering the zoom factor accordingly.\cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl \{bmc bm45.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \fs28 \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\fs24 \tab The dual magnification object is a 'hollow' object, this means that in \uldb \b Mouse Control Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Mouse_Control_Mode}\plain\fs24 , the mouse can be clicked and dragged inside the dual magnification object to adjust any parameters assigned to the mouse such as magnification and focus. When selected, the cursor changes shape to a mini dual magnification graphic \{bmc bm46.bmp\} when the mouse is over an adjustable part of the dual magnification object. 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Ellipse Annotation}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} I3XG.D}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 \{bmc bm18.bmp\}  Ellipse Annotation
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par Adds an ellipse to the overlay plane. The ellipse can be transparent or filled with a pattern.
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx9065\cellx13255\pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select the \b Ellipse Annotation Icon\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Point to the \b place\plain\fs24  where you want to start the ellipse and \ul drag\plain\fs24 {\v DRAG@Sem.HLP} to the place where you want to end the ellipse.
\par\intbl \{bmc bm31.bmp\}\tab Holding down \b <SHIFT>\plain\fs24  while dragging the mouse generates a \b circle\plain\fs24  around the centre.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab The ellipse will be shown at the selected place of the Image Window and can be dragged into the required \b position\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl\tx355 \par\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \par\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm40.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab The \ul handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles@Sem.HLP} of the ellipse can be picked up and be used for reseizing the ellipse.
\par \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Holding the \b <SHIFT>\plain\fs24  key down whilst adjusting a selection handle will cause the ellipse object to snap to a circle. 
\par \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Using the \b <SHIFT> \plain\fs24 key and the diagonal selection handles will shrink and grow the object from its centre point.
\par 
\par The coordinates of the ellipse are displayed in the \uldb Status Bar\plain\fs24 {\v RDJHU5@Sem.HLP}. \{bmc bm47.bmp\}
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 (From corner X/Y to opposite corner X/Y, centre X/Y, size X/Y)
\par 
\par See also\tab \uldb \b Properties of Ellipse Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Ellipse_Annotation}\plain\fs24 
\par 
\par \page
