{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} JPEG}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} JPEG}
\pard\tx355 \fs36\cf1 JPEG\b 
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\f2\fs20 
\par \pard\tx355 \fs24 (pronounced "jay-peg") is a standardized compression method for full-color
\par \pard\tx355 and gray-scale images.  JPEG is designed to handle "real-world" scenes,
\par \pard\tx355 for example scanned photographs. \fs20 
\par \pard\tx355 \fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 JPEG is lossy, meaning that the output image is not necessarily identical to
\par \pard\tx355 the input image.  Hence you should not use JPEG if you have to have identical
\par \pard\tx355 output bits.  However, on typical real-world images, very good compression
\par \pard\tx355 levels can be obtained with no visible change, and amazingly high compression
\par \pard\tx355 is possible if you can tolerate a low-quality image.  You can trade off image
\par \pard\tx355 quality against file size by adjusting the compressor's "\ul quality\plain\f2\fs24 {\v JPEG_Quality}" setting.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} JPEG Quality}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} JPEG_Quality}
\pard \f1\b\fs36\cf1 JPEG Quality
\par \plain\f2\fs24 The quality switch lets you trade off compressed file size against quality of
\par the reconstructed image: the higher the quality setting, the larger the JPEG
\par file, and the closer the output image will be to the original input.  Normally
\par you want to use the lowest quality setting (smallest file) that decompresses
\par into something visually indistinguishable from the original image.  For this
\par purpose the quality setting should be between 50 and 95; the default of 75 is
\par \pard often about right.  If you see defects at quality 75, then go up 5 or 10
\par counts at a time until you are happy with the output image.  (The optimal
\par setting will vary from one image to another.)
\par 
\par In the other direction, quality values below 50 will produce very small files
\par of low image quality.  Settings around 5 to 10 might be useful in preparing an
\par index of a large image library, for example.  \fs20 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Keyboard Adjustment}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Keyboard_Adjustment}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \f1\b\fs36\cf1 \{bmc bm28.bmp\}  Keyboard Adjustment
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par When an object is \uldb \b selected\plain\b\fs24 {\v Select_Mode}\plain\fs24 , it will display selection \ul handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles@Sem.HLP}. To cycle through the currently selected selection handle, press the \b <Space>\plain\fs24  key. When no handles are selected, the object is in move mode.
\par 
\par At any point, press an\b  arrow key\plain\fs24  on the keyboard \{bmc bm73.bmp\}\{bmc bm74.bmp\}\{bmc bm75.bmp\}\{bmc bm76.bmp\} to either \ul drag\plain\fs24 {\v DRAG@Sem.HLP} a selection handle, or to move the object, depending on the currently selected handle.
\par 
\par  If the object does not move or size, try \ul \b\ul clicking\plain\b\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  on it with the mouse to ensure that the object is both selected, and that Image Manager has the keyboard focus.
\par \pard 
\par In some special cases the \b <SHIFT> key\plain\fs24  \{bmc bm77.bmp\} can be used whilst dragging selection handles:
\par 
\par \pard\li355 \uldb \b Rectangle\plain\b\fs24 {\v Rectangle_Annotation}\plain\fs24  and \uldb \b Ellipse\plain\b\fs24 {\v Ellipse_Annotation}\plain\fs24  : Shift key 'snaps' width and height to create a \b square\plain\fs24  or \b circle\plain\i\fs24 .
\par 
\par \uldb \plain\b\uldb\fs24 Bitmap\plain\b\fs24 {\v Insert_User_Bitmap_or_Metafile}\plain\fs24  : Maintains X and Y axis aspect ratio when sizing
\par 
\par \uldb \b Sticky Panel\plain\b\fs24 {\v Sticky_Panel_Annotation}\plain\fs24  : Scales panel as in Bitmap, but also scales objects 'stuck' to it
\par \pard 
\par In some special cases the \b <CTRL> key\plain\fs24  can be used whilst dragging selection handles:
\par 
\par \pard\li355 This will step the X or Y axis by the amount specified in the \uldb \b Alignment Grid Spacing\plain\b\fs24 {\v Alignment_Grid_Spacing}\plain\fs24  Dialog. This is convenient for sizing an object quickly with the keyboard.
