{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Multiple Object Selection
\par \pard \plain\fs28    \fs24 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx8495\cellx12685\pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b\fs28 \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs28 \tab \fs24 This can be achieved by \ul clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} outside a group of objects with the left mouse button. Whilst holding down the left mouse button, \ul drag\plain\fs24 {\v DRAG@Sem.HLP} the mouse over the objects. A 'rubber rectangle' will follow the mouse, and any objects intersecting the rectangle will be selected and display their selection handles. 
\par\intbl \b\fs28 \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs28 \tab \fs24 To add or remove objects from a multiple selection list click on the object whilst holding down \b <CTRL>\plain\fs24 . 
\par\intbl \fs28 \cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl \{bmc bm96.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard \{bmc bm4.bmp\} \fs24 Note that there are two main differences in multi-select mode :\fs28 
\par \pard\li355 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \fs24 \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\tab The objects can only be moved as a group, not sized
\par 
\par \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\tab Right clicking to give a properties menu on any object, gives a properties menu with an aggregate of all of the properties available in the group, even though some objects would not usually have that property (e.g. A \uldb \b line object\plain\b\fs24 {\v Line_Annotation}\plain\fs24  has the ability to have arrow heads, a \uldb \b rectangle\plain\b\fs24 {\v Rectangle_Annotation}\plain\fs24  doesn't, however when the two objects are selected together, the single properties menu displays this option- this is not a bug, as only those applicable properties for the selected objects can be changed).
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par Multiple select mode is a convenient way of grouping objects and changing for example their \uldb \b brush\plain\b\fs24 {\v Selection_of_Brush_Settings}\plain\fs24  or \uldb \b font\plain\b\fs24 {\v Selection_of_Font__Style_and_Colour}\plain\fs24  properties all in one operation.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 In all cases, clicking on the image removes a current selection.
\par \page
