{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Reasons for missing/greyed Functions}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Reasons_for_missing_greyed_Functions}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Reasons for missing/greyed Functions
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par This topic lists a number of reasons why either some \uldb \b Annotation Toolbar\plain\b\fs24 {\v Calling_the_Annotation_Toolbar}\plain\fs24  functions are either greyed out, or are not visible :
\par 
\par \pard\li715\fi-355\tx715 \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\tab The required option may not be licenced for use on the machine that the software is running
\par 
\par \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\tab The maximum number of times a function could be used has been reached. For example only one instance of full screen \uldb \b Height Measurement\plain\b\fs24 {\v Height_Measurement} \plain\fs24 cursors and \uldb \b Width Measurement\plain\b\fs24 {\v Width_Measurement}\plain\fs24  cursors is allowed.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\tab The system is not running in the correct context at present for the function to be valid. For example, no more items to \uldb \b delete\plain\b\fs24 {\v Deleting_of_Annotation}\plain\fs24  or nothing to \uldb \b undo\plain\b\fs24 {\v Undo_Last_Edit}.\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Recover All Offscreen}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Recover_All_Offscreen}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Recover All Offscreen
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par This function moves any off-screen objects to the screen centre.
\par 
\par To see if there are any off-screen objects check the \uldb \b Annotation Panel\plain\b\fs24 {\v Calling_the_Annotation_Toolbar}\plain\fs24  Caption (the blue bar on top of the panel):
\par 
\par When objects are off-screen the caption displays the number of objects with the code \b O:n\plain\fs24  where \b n\plain\fs24  is the number of off-screen objects placed outside the 1024x768 display area. 
\par 
\par This may happen when for example an object is \ul dragged\plain\fs24 {\v DRAG@Sem.HLP} off-screen, and cannot be selected to drag back into the display area.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par One object will therefore read \b O:1\plain\fs24  and so on.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab When the objects are moved to the screen centre they may overlap and hide each other there.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Rectangle Annotation}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Rectangle_Annotation}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 \{bmc bm17.bmp\}  Rectangle Annotation
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 Adds a rectangle to the overlay plane. The rectangle can be transparent or filled with a pattern.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx7935\cellx12115\pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select the \b Rectangle Annotation Icon\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Point to the \b place\plain\fs24  where you want to start the rectangle and \ul drag\plain\fs24 {\v DRAG@Sem.HLP} to the place where you want to end the rectangle.
\par\intbl \{bmc bm31.bmp\}\tab Holding down \b <SHIFT>\plain\fs24  while dragging the mouse generates a \b square\plain\fs24  around the centre.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab The rectangle will be shown at the selected place of the Image Window and can be dragged into the required \b position\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl\tx355 \par\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 \par\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm106.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab The \ul Handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles@Sem.HLP} of the rectangle can be picked up and be used for reseizing the rectangle.
\par \tab Holding the\b  <SHIFT>\plain\fs24  key down whilst adjusting a selection handle will cause the rectangle object to snap to a square. 
\par \tab Using the \b <SHIFT>\plain\fs24  key and the diagonal selection handles will shrink and grow the object from its centre point.
\par 
\par The coordinates of the rectangle are displayed in the \uldb Status Bar\plain\fs24 {\v RDJHU5@Sem.HLP}. \{bmc bm107.bmp\} 
\par (From corner X/Y to opposite corner X/Y, centre X/Y, size X/Y)
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 Also see\b  \tab \uldb Properties of Rectangle Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Properties_of_Rectangle_Annotation}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Reduced Format display of results}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Reduced_Format_display_of_results}
\pard\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 Reduced Format display of results
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 By default the results of a measurement object are displayed on an associated \uldb Results Panel\plain\fs24 {\v Results_Parameter_Selections}. The position of the Panel is unrestricted with respect to its measurement object. To enable the user to identify which measurement object is associated with which results panel each measurement object has one or more lables (e.g. Pa 1).
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 An Alternative Reduced format is used when no results are selected on the results panel.
\par \pard\tx355 In this format a label is not displayed and the value of the default parameter is displayed attached to the measurement object
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \page
