\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Reduced Raster Object}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Reduced_Raster_Object}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Reduced Raster Object
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par This object is a 'mode graphic' and simply exists to depict boundaries of the SEM \uldb \b Reduced Raster Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v BFKLC0@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 . 
\par 
\par \{bmc bm27.bmp\} This function can be selected from the SEM \uldb \b Menu Bar\plain\b\fs24 {\v 8ZP0K4@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 . 
\par 
\par The colour of this graphic can be changed from the \uldb \b Annotation Settings Popup Menu\plain\b\fs24 {\v Annotation_Settings}\plain\fs24  under \uldb \b Selection of Pen Settings\plain\b\fs24 {\v Selection_of_Pen_Settings}\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par The \uldb \b selected\plain\b\fs24 {\v Object_Selection}\plain\fs24  object can be reseized by \ul dragging\plain\fs24 {\v DRAG@Sem.HLP} its selection\ul  handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles@Sem.HLP} to the desired object size, or moved by \ul clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} on one of the objects lines and dragging the mouse. After the mouse button is released, the SEM Reduced Raster Mode parameters are set to these new values.
\par \pard 
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx11265\pard\intbl 
\par\intbl When selected, this object can be \uldb \b deleted\plain\b\fs24 {\v Deleting_of_Annotation}\plain\fs24  by using its \uldb \b Properties\plain\b\fs24 {\v Objects_Properties_Menu}\plain\fs24  popup menu as shown right. Deleting this object sets the SEM to \uldb \b Normal Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v 4LTP_XJ@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 .
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm108.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \fs28 
\par \{bmc bm4.bmp\}\fs24 \tab The reduced raster object is a 'hollow' object, this means that in \uldb \b Mouse Control Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Mouse_Control_Mode}\plain\fs24 , the mouse can be clicked and dragged inside the reduced raster object to adjust any parameters assigned to the mouse such as magnification and focus. When selected, the cursor changes shape to a mini reduced raster graphic \{bmc bm109.bmp\} when the mouse is over an \i adjustable\plain\fs24  part of the reduced raster object. \fs28 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Reset Drawing Properties}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Reset_Drawing_Properties}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Reset Drawing Properties
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par The properties of the annotation object may be reset either to the properties which have been selected by the user and were stored before o to the default settings.
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx7935\cellx12965\pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Click to one of the \ul Radio Buttons\plain\fs24 {\v 2R6X4F@Sem.HLP} to make your selection.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Click on \b <OK>\plain\fs24  to reset the properties.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab \b <Cancel>\plain\fs24  or closing the window with \{bmc bm24.bmp\} will leave the panel without resetting the properties.
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl \{bmc bm110.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par The objects properties will either revert to those set up via the \uldb \b Annotation Settings\plain\b\fs24 {\v Annotation_Settings}\plain\fs24  Popup Menu, or to a default selection defined by Image Manager for the specific object being reset.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Reset User Settings}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Reset_User_Settings}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Reset Standard Annotation User Settings
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par This function when selected from the \uldb \b Standard Annotation Options Tab\plain\b\fs24 {\v The_Standard_Annotation_Options_Tab}\plain\fs24 , will revert the standard user settings stored in the \uldb \b Annotation Preferences File\plain\b\fs24 {\v User_Preferences_File}\plain\fs24  back to default factory settings.
\par 
\par \fs16 \{bmc bm4.bmp\}  Note that there is no undo option available once the user has confirmed this action. If you select this option by mistake, you may \uldb load\plain\fs16 {\v Load_User_Settings} any previously \uldb stored annotation preferences\plain\fs16 {\v Save_User_Settings} back from disk.\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Results Panel Parameters}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Results_Panel_Parameters}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Results Panel Parameters
\par \pard \plain\fs24 When double ckicking on a measurement object, or by selection of its \b Measurement Results Panel Properties\plain\fs24  Item, the following dialog appears. \b Note \plain\fs24 Deselecting all parameters invokes the \uldb \b Reduced Format display of results\plain\b\fs24 {\v Reduced_Format_display_of_results}\plain\fs24 
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6795\cellx12115\pard\intbl The \b Current Selection\plain\fs24  list box displays the results to be placed into the measurement objects results panel.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The \b Available parameters\plain\fs24  list box displays the parameters that can be added to the results panel, but are not currently displayed.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The default for each instance of a measurement object is to have all of its associated measurement parameters currently selected.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl Double click on a parameter in either list box to move it to the opposite list box. Alternatively, select the desired parameter and press the button associated with its list box to move it.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl Note : Each instance of a measurement object has its \i own\plain\fs24  settings regarding results parameters. There are usually two instances available of each measurement object, with the exception of the point to point markers, and the adjustable height and width cursor objects, which can be licenced (advanced measurement) to allow upto ten instances of each of these object to be created.\cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm86.bmp\}
\par\intbl \cell\intbl\row
\pard \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Results Parameter Selections}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Results_Parameter_Selections}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Results Parameter Selections
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par Each measurement object that has a \uldb \b Measurement Results Panel\plain\b\fs24 {\v Measurement_Results_Panel}\plain\fs24 , such as the \uldb \b Point to Point Measurement Object\plain\b\fs24 {\v Point_to_Point}\plain\fs24  also has an associated set of results that can be added to the measurement results panel when the object is created. If no results are selected, then a results panel for that particular object will not be created and the \uldb \b reduced format\plain\b\fs24 {\v Reduced_Format_display_of_results}\plain\fs24  used.
\par 
\par Each instance of measurement object has its own set of selected parameters associated with it. Parameters are selected by using the
\par \pard  \uldb \b Measurement Results Selection Dialog\plain\b\fs24 {\v Measurement_Results_Selection_Dialog}\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par These settings are automatically saved in the users directory into a \uldb \b CURRENT.ANP\plain\b\fs24 {\v User_Preferences_File}\plain\fs24  file.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Retrospective Measurement}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Retrospective_Measurement}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Retrospective Measurement
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par By Retrospective Measurement we mean measurements performed on an image loaded from a TIFF file.
\par 
\par Provided the file has stored within it zone data the measurement objects will use the saved operating mode and pixel sizes to generate correct measurement values. The Zone magnification will will also be correct and will adjust for the current output device.
\par 
\par \b Normal analogue and digital annotation objects should not be used as they will display the current values and not those relating to the TIFF image.
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24  
\par \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\tab \b WARNING: \uldb MEASUREMENTS WHEN IMPORTED IMAGE IS NOT FULL SIZE\plain\b\fs24 {\v Measurement_Warning}\plain\fs24  
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} SEM  Menu Bar}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} SEM__Menu_Bar}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 SEM  Menu Bar
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par The SEM UIF application is tightly integrated with the Image Manager, in that a number of Image Manager functions are directly available from the SEM \uldb \b Menu Bar\plain\b\fs24 {\v 8ZP0K4@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  . The interactions from the Image Manager are discussed:
\par 
\par \pard\li715\fi-355\tx715 \fs28 \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\tab \fs24 The \uldb \b File Menu\plain\b\fs24 {\v 6XNXVL@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  allows for the \uldb \b Export\plain\b\fs24 {\v Export_TIFF}\plain\fs24  or \uldb \b Import of TIFF\plain\b\fs24 {\v Import_TIFF}\plain\fs24  format Image files, and for \uldb \b Annotation Loading\plain\b\fs24 {\v Loading_Annotation}\plain\fs24  and \uldb \b Saving\plain\b\fs24 {\v Saving_Annotation}\plain\fs24  to be actioned.
\par \fs28 
\par \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\tab \fs24 From the\fs28  \uldb \b\fs24 Edit Menu\plain\b\fs24 {\v 28IFRD5@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 ,\fs28  \fs24 the \uldb \b Annotation Toolbar\plain\b\fs24 {\v Calling_the_Annotation_Toolbar}\plain\fs24  can be displayed by selecting \uldb \b Annotation\plain\b\fs24 {\v 3KJ6VR@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 . 
\par \tab This menu option also allows for \uldb \b Annotation Text Objects\plain\b\fs24 {\v Text_Annotation}\plain\fs24  and \uldb \b Point to Point Measurement\plain\b\fs24 {\v Point_to_Point}\plain\fs24  Objects to be created directly.\fs28 
\par \pard\tx715 \fs24 
\par \pard\li715\fi-355\tx715 \fs28 \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\tab \fs24 The \uldb \b View Menu\plain\b\fs24 {\v 6Y3XP3@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs28  \fs24 allows the loading of the current or a default \uldb \b Data Zone\plain\b\fs24 {\v Data_Zone}\plain\fs24  into the \uldb \b Image Window\plain\b\fs24 {\v GIRNF0@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 . 
\par \tab Note that when a default datazone is loaded, the \b CURRENT.ADZ\plain\fs24  datazone in the user directory is overwritten. A prompt is displayed however to confirm this option. This menu also allows the creation of a \uldb \b Crosshairs Object\plain\b\fs24 {\v Crosshairs_Object}\plain\fs24  and a \uldb \b Graticule Object\plain\b\fs24 {\v Graticule_Object}\plain\fs24 . The parameters for \uldb \b Graticule Spacing\plain\b\fs24 {\v 199M5I2@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  are also available through the menu item. 
\par \pard\li715\fi-355\tx715 \tab The \b Hide\plain\fs24  and \b Unhide\plain\fs24  menu items allow various categories of annotation object to either be shown or \uldb \b hidden\plain\b\fs24 {\v Hidden_Objects}\plain\fs24 . When displayed, the Annotation Toolbar will display a \b H:n\plain\fs24  in its title caption where \b n \plain\fs24 represents the number of objects currently hidden.
\par \pard\tx715 
\par \pard\li715\fi-355\tx715 \fs28 \{bmc bm21.bmp\}\tab \fs24 The \uldb \b Scanning Menu\plain\b\fs24 {\v 215WPY_@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs28  \fs24 deals mainly with changes in \uldb \b SEM Operating Modes\plain\b\fs24 {\v SEM_Operating_Mode}\plain\fs24 . 
\par \tab As a result of a SEM operating mode change, associalted \uldb \b Image Manager Objects\plain\b\fs24 {\v Image_Manager_Objects}\plain\fs24  may also be created. Tthese are used to graphically interact with the operating mode parameters :
\par 
\par \pard\tx355 \tab \tab \uldb \b Reduced Raster\plain\b\fs24 {\v Reduced_Raster_Object}\tab \uldb Split Screen\plain\b\fs24 {\v Split_Screen_Object}\tab \tab \uldb Dual Magnification\plain\b\fs24 {\v Dual_Magnification_Object}\tab \tab \uldb Spot Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Spot_Mode_Object}\tab \tab \uldb Line Scan\plain\b\fs24 {\v Line_Scan_Object}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
