{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Software Principles}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Software_Principles}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Software Principles
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par The \b LEO-32 Image Manager\plain\fs24  which handles Annotation and Measurement is a special application that co-exists with the SEM User Interface (SEM UIF). It acts as an \i overlay\plain\fs24  on top of the SEM Image, and allows various image related functions to be actioned easily. Typical functions would be those that add image annotation and measurement objects, save images, change SEM operating modes etc.
\par 
\par Unlike most windows applications, when the SEM UIF window is moved, the image portion or 'Client Area' to use Window terminology; remains static along with the overlay provided by Image Manager. The SEM UIF Client Area and Image Manager overlay can in this way be seen as a \i viewport\plain\fs24  on the Image.
\par \pard 
\par The Image Manager is controlled mainly by a simple 'selection and execution' process using the mouse. The user 'selects' a toolbar or menu function and 'executes' its features by clicking and dragging the mouse over the desired area of the viewport. Where possible, some additional keyboard short-cuts have also been implemented for use with Image Manager.
\par As Image Manager is object oriented, all of its functions can be classed or sub-classed into \i Image Manager Objects\plain\fs24 . An object can be anything from a measurement tool, to a piece of text, to a file selection area.
\par \pard 
\par Finally, Image Manager works with a standard \uldb \b co-ordinate system\plain\b\fs24 {\v Co_ordinate_System}\plain\fs24  and  a \uldb \b standard measurement system\plain\b\fs24 {\v Standard_Measurement_System}\plain\fs24  that is applied to all objects.
\par 
\par The Image Manager, where possible, is logically partitioned into a number of discrete sections, each having one of two goals : -To select a specific function or action. -To apply a specific action in the context of the selected function. Selection of functions can be achieved by making selections from  :
\par 
\par \pard The \uldb \b Annotation Toolbar\plain\b\fs24 {\v Calling_the_Annotation_Toolbar}\plain\fs24 , the SEM \uldb \b Menu Bar\plain\b\fs24 {\v 8ZP0K4@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24  and the Image Manager \uldb \b Context Sensitive Popup Menu\plain\b\fs24 {\v Context_Sensitive_Popup_Menu}\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par The contents of the Popup Menu are determined by the current \uldb \b operating mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Image_Manager_Operating_Mode}\plain\fs24  of the Image Manager. Wherever possible, help text is displayed in the SEM UIF \uldb \b Status Bar Message Area\plain\b\fs24 {\v Status_Bar_Message_Area}\plain\fs24 . Objects can easily be \uldb \b created\plain\b\fs24 {\v Creation_of_Objects}\plain\fs24  or \uldb \b selected\plain\b\fs24 {\v Object_Selection}\plain\fs24  by using the Annotation Toolbar functions.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Special Panel Settings}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Special_Panel_Settings}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Special Panel Settings
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \{bmc bm10.bmp\}\tab \b Select the Sticky Panel\plain\fs24  by \ul clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} with the left mouse button on it. The selected object displays \ul handles\plain\fs24 {\v Handles@Sem.HLP}.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm8.bmp\}\tab \b Open the Properties Dialog\plain\fs24  by clicking with the right mouse button.
\par 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select \b Lock this Panel\plain\fs24  or \b Unlock this Panel.\plain\fs24  The selected mode is indicated with \{bmc bm23.bmp\}.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm4.bmp\}\tab When the panel is locked, the annotation objects which are placed on the panel cannot be selected, sized or changed in any way until this setting is unchecked.
\par \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select \b Make Panel a Data Zone\plain\fs24  or \b Don't make Panel a Data Zone\plain\fs24 . The selected mode is indicated with \{bmc bm23.bmp\}. This is simply an internal tag that allows the panel to be identified as a DataZone.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par \{bmc bm4.bmp\}\tab Datazones are handled differently from normal annotation objects: They can be individually hidden and unhidden from top menu functions on the Menu Bar \uldb \b Menu View\plain\b\fs24 {\v 6Y3XP3@Sem.HLP}\plain\fs24 . Also RESET does not remove them
\par 
\par \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab To save a panel as a DataZone, first ensure that the \b Make Panel a Data Zone\plain\fs24  option is selected. Secondly, ensure that only the panel to be saved is selected. Finally, when the file save dialog appears, ensure that the \i Save Selected Annotation Only\plain\fs24  flag is checked and that the file extension is .ADZ e.g \i MyDataZone.ADZ\plain\fs24 .
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 
\par \b \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Usually the user alters the current DataZone to their own requirements, these changes are automatically remembered until the Default DataZone is re-loaded. If you have created your own DataZone panel however, and do not wish to edit the current DataZone, then select \b Make Current Data Zone\plain\fs24  to overwrite the current DataZone with your new settings.
\par \page
